<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qingquan Li
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>This work was completed while Qingquan Li was at Ant Group.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shaoyu Dou
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kailai Shao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Chao Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haixiang Hu
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The remarkable performance of Large Language Models (LLMs) gives rise to“LLM-as-a-Judge”, where LLMs are employed as evaluators for complex tasks.
Moreover, it has been widely adopted across fields such as Natural Language Processing (NLP), preference learning, and various specific domains.
However, there are various biases within LLM-as-a-Judge, which adversely affect the fairness and reliability of judgments.
Current research on evaluating or mitigating bias in LLM-as-a-Judge predominantly focuses on comparison-based evaluations, while systematic investigations into bias in scoring-based evaluations remain limited.
Therefore, we define <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">scoring bias in LLM-as-a-Judge</span> as the scores differ when scoring judge models are bias-related perturbed, and provide a well-designed framework to comprehensively evaluate scoring bias.
We augment existing LLM-as-a-Judge benchmarks through data synthesis to construct our evaluation dataset and design multi-faceted evaluation metrics.
Our experimental results demonstrate that the scoring stability of existing judge models is disrupted by scoring biases.
Further exploratory experiments and discussions provide valuable insights into the design of scoring prompt templates and the mitigation of scoring biases on aspects such as score rubrics, score IDs, and reference answer selection.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
        In the interest of protecting our intellectual property, specific technical details have been redacted from this preliminary version. The complete and unredacted manuscript is forthcoming.</span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="id3" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Ant Group</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Hangzhou, China</p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">qingquanl97@outlook.com</p>
<p id="p1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">{doushaoyu.dsy, kailai.skl, chixi.cc, zengxian}@antgroup.com</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs), with outstanding performance, have been widely applied to Natural Language Generation (NLG) tasks such as summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> question answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robinson, Rytting, and Wingate 2022</a>)</cite>, and domain-specific dialogues <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singhal et al. 2025</a>)</cite>.
In these tasks, the model’s responses require find-grained evaluation of various aspects such as quality, factuality, and instruction-following, thereby providing valuable insights for model improvement.
Traditional statistical NLG evaluation metrics, such as BLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papineni et al. 2002</a>)</cite> and ROUGE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin 2004</a>)</cite>, evaluate the generated text by n-gram overlap between the text and the golden answer.
The above methods exhibit two primary limitations:
(1) They focus solely on the similarity between the generated text and the golden answer, not quantifying intrinsic attributes of the text such as writing style, factuality, and educational value.
(2) The resulting metrics lack interpretability, making it difficult to provide guidance for the model upgrade.
There is an urgent need for fine-grained, multifaceted, and interpretable evaluation metrics and methods tailored to NLG tasks and LLM applications.
With remarkable capabilities in instruction understanding and response generation, LLMs can provide diverse forms of evaluation output, such as scoring, comparison, and ranking.
In addition, they can conduct evaluations along specified dimensions and generate critics for judgments, thus enhancing the interpretability of the evaluation.
This line of research, referred to as “LLM-as-a-Judge” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>, has recently emerged as a prominent focus.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22316v1/framework_arXiv_v1.png" id="Sx1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:558/197;" width="558" height="197" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Our evaluation framework to measure the scoring bias in LLM-as-a-Judge.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Robustness is a critical factor in ensuring the fairness and reliability of LLM judgments, which requires that, under identical judge rubrics, LLM judges should provide consistent judgments for the same evaluation target.
However, recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. 2024</a>)</cite> indicates that LLM judges are subject to various biases, which undermine the self-consistency of their judgments.
Many studies have sought to identify the types of bias that shift in judgments.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite> finds that the judgments of the candidate responses can be easily hacked by altering their position in the context.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wataoka, Takahashi, and Ri 2024</a>)</cite> measures the self-preference bias in LLM-as-a-Judge, which refers to the tendency of LLM judges to overestimate the quality of their own outputs.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. 2024</a>)</cite> considers length bias as LLM judges favor longer responses or shorter responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although existing studies systematically investigated various biases in LLM-as-a-Judge, several pressing issues remain to be addressed.
Firstly, while some work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> has addressed biases such as positional bias and refinement awareness in scoring-based LLM judges, many existing research remains focused on biases in comparative LLM judges.
Existing work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> attempts to investigate specific biases in scoring tasks, such as self-enhancement bias and refinement-aware bias.
However, there is still a lack of comprehensive research on biases in scoring-based LLM-as-a-Judge.
Secondly, the biases defined in existing work are predominantly related to the attributes or presentation forms of the evaluation targets, such as position, length, and response model.
However, variation inherent in the prompt of LLM-based judge itself may also impact the self-consistency of judgments, which lacks a systematic investigation in current research.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To solve the above problems, we propose <span id="Sx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">scoring bias</span>, defined as the scoring shifts of LLM-as-a-Judge under perturbations of the scoring prompt.
In this work, we propose three types of scoring bias: score rubrics order bias, score IDs bias, and reference answer score bias, and design a framework to systematically evaluate those biases.
We select four existing LLM-as-a-Judge benchmarks as evaluation datasets and develop a series of metrics to quantify the robustness of LLM judges against the score bias.
Our key contributions can be summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We pioneer a systematic evaluation of bias in scoring-based LLM-as-a-Judge with definition of three types of scoring bias and five perturbation methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through automatic data synthesis, we expand existing LLM-as-a-Judge benchmarks into our evaluation dataset without human labor costs. Furthermore, we evaluate scoring bias from multiple perspectives through a comprehensive design of evaluation metrics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our comprehensive experiments and in-depth analysis find that scoring bias affects the robustness and reliability of various existing judge models. We also provide valuable recommendations for the design of scoring prompts and the mitigation of scoring bias.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Related Work</h2>

<section id="Sx2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">LLM-as-a-Judge</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. 2024</a>)</cite> defines LLM-as-a-Judge as the task in which an LLM generates an evaluation result in any form given input data and context.
Common forms of LLM judgment include score generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, pairwise comparison <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, ranking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li, Patel, and Du 2023</a>)</cite>, multiple-choice selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>, and critic generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.
Improvement strategies for LLM-as-a-Judge include meticulous design of evaluation prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, improvement of LLM’s abilities such as fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024c</a>)</cite>, multi-LLM collaboration pipelines such as feedback mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, and output optimization strategies such as multi-output ensemble <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sottana et al. 2023</a>)</cite>.
The performance of LLM-as-a-Judge is usually measured using metrics that represent its alignment with human annotators or advanced LLM judgments such as agreement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakur et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, Cohen’s Kappa, and Spearman’s correlation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. 2023</a>)</cite>.
LLM-as-a-Judge is widely applied to evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin and Chen 2023</a>)</cite>, alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>, and other specific domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ryu et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Babaei and Giudici 2024</a>)</cite>.
LLM-as-a-Judge faces challenges in bias and vulnerability, which significantly compromise the fairness and reliability of LLM judges. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024a</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> defines the prompt for LLM judges as three components: system instruction, question, and response.
Based on this, they define bias in LLM-as-a-Judge as that the judgments differ when system instruction or responses is bias-related modified.
Common types of bias in LLM-as-a-Judge include position bias, length bias, self-enhancement bias, etc.
Those biases are often incorporated into the evaluation scope of existing LLM-as-a-Judge benchmarks. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. 2024</a>)</cite>
Currently, the types of bias still remain a focal point of investigation, and numerous benchmarks are proposed to evaluate bias in LLM-as-a-Judge.
OffsetBias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. 2024</a>)</cite> points out six types of bias in LLM-as-a-Judge including length bias, concreteness bias, empty reference bias, and so on.
CALM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> quantifies and analyzes 12 types of bias in LLM judges.
However, the biases defined in current work are predominantly focused on comparative evaluation tasks, with a lack of systematic research and definitions specifically addressing biases in scoring-based evaluation tasks.
Similar to research on adversarial attacks, the evaluation metrics for bias in LLM-as-a-Judge are often based on the consistency between bias-related modified judgments and the original judgments, or on alignment with golden answers.
Some studies focus on mitigating bias in LLM-as-a-Judge and aim to develop more robust LLM judges.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024c</a>)</cite> introduces Pairwise-Preference Search (PAIRS) to mitigate biases in LLM evaluators.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite> proposes three methods to calibrate positional bias in LLM-as-a-Judge, including multiple evidence calibration, balanced position calibration, and human-in-the-loop calibration.
Our evaluation results on scoring bias will serve as a reference to guide future research in mitigating scoring biases in judge models and enhancing the robustness of scoring-based evaluations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Evaluation Method</h2>

<figure id="Sx3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22316v1/prompt_arXiv_v1.svg" id="Sx3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:698/633;" width="698" height="633"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The scoring prompt template with four components from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. 2024c</a>)</cite>. The <span id="Sx3.F2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red text</span> represents the components may be perturbed.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows our evaluation framework, inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="Sx3.T1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:495.8pt;height:86.4pt;vertical-align:-39.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(72.3pt,-12.6pt) scale(1.41192047529423,1.41192047529423) ;">
