</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Correlations between LLM judgement pipeline and human when generating golden scores. <math id="Sx3.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient. <math id="Sx3.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is Pearson’s correlation coefficient. The GPT-4.1 and GPT-4o scores are generated by our work, the GPT-4 scores are derived from the original datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The detailed statics of our selected datasets are shown in Table <a href="#Sx3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Evaluation Method ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
For advanced LLM scores, the above datasets have evaluation results obtained from GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2023b</a>)</cite>.
Due to the earlier release of GPT-4, we use more recent models GPT-4.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2025</a>)</cite> and GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2024</a>)</cite> to obtain golden scores.
Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. 2024c</a>)</cite>, we repeat the scoring three times through a judge prompt with a reference answer that marked a score of 5 and make a majority vote to obtain the final score from LLM evaluators.
Table <a href="#Sx3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Datasets ‣ Evaluation Method ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the correlation between advanced LLM scores and human scores.
The results show that the GPT-4.1 scores exhibit the highest correlation with human scores, so we select the GPT-4.1 scores as the golden LLM scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Each instance in BiGGen Bench and FLASK is accompanied by a reference answer with score 5.
To measure the impact of the reference answer score on the judgment robustness, it is necessary to additionally generate reference answers with scores ranging from 1 to 4.
We propose a generation-review pipeline to synthesize reference answers for each sample in FLASK, as shown in Figure <a href="#Sx3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Metrics ‣ Evaluation Method ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Given an instruction and score rubrics, the response model generates the response according to the description from score 1 to 4.
Then a review model checks whether the response matches the corresponding score description.
If match, we accept the generated responses; otherwise, we rerun the pipeline until a match is achieved.
We alternately apply GPT-4.1 and GPT-4o as response model and review model in the pipeline.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Metrics</h3>

<figure id="Sx3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22316v1/ref_ans_gen.png" id="Sx3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/345;" width="698" height="345" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The proposed generation-review pipeline for synthesizing reference answers.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the scoring bias in LLM-as-a-Judge, we consider the impact or perturbation methods on scoring accuracy and scoring tendency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
