<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Correlation between golden scores and judge model scores with bias in score rubrics order and score IDs. For each judge model, the first row is the metrics without bias, the remaining rows is the metrics under different types of bias. The differences between results with and without bias reflect the extent to which bias impacts the scoring robustness. <span id="Sx4.T3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DeepSeek-V3</span> is DeepSeek-V3-671B. <span id="Sx4.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mistral-24B</span> is Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct. <math id="Sx4.T3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient. <math id="Sx4.T3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is Pearson’s correlation coefficient.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Setup</h3>

<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Judge Models.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024b</a>)</cite> has reached a consensus that judge models should be stronger models to ensure evaluation reliability. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>
However, as an exploratory study, we think that capability and size of the judge model should also be considered as influence factors.
Therefore, we select diverse judge models that include closed-source, open-source, large-size, and small-size models.
They are GPT-4o, DeepSeek-V3-671B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>, Qwen3-32B, Qwen3-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, and Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">MistralAI 2025</a>)</cite>.
Those judge models we selected differ from the models used to generate response in the evaluation datasets, thus mitigating potential self-enhancement bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Judge Prompt.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The prompt we use for scoring-based judgment is shown in Figure <a href="#Sx3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Evaluation Method ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. 2024c</a>)</cite>.
The unperturbed prompt is the one with ascending score rubrics, Arabic numeral score IDs, and no reference answer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Hyperparameters.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As our goals for scoring bias evaluations, we aim to ensure the stability of the model’s generative capabilities.
Therefore, we set the judge models’ temperature to 0 while scoring.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Main Results</h3>

<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Judge models are sensitive to scoring biases in input prompt templates.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#Sx4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Figure <a href="#Sx4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Judge models are sensitive to scoring biases in input prompt templates. ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show the correlation of judge model scores and golden scores on all perturbation methods.
All judge models we evaluated, even for an advanced model such as GPT-4, fluctuations in score results are observed under different biases, indicating that scoring bias has a significant impact on the robustness of LLM-as-a-Judge.
Furthermore, the impact exhibits certain differences depending on the choice of judge model:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) The capability and size of the judge models are critical factors influencing the robustness against scoring bias.</span>
Consistent with existing consensus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>, our experimental results indicate that judge models with advanced capability and larger size tend to exhibit higher reliability on scoring.
As shown in Table <a href="#Sx4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, for example, across all perturbation methods and datasets, the fluctuation in correlation coefficients between GPT-4o and the golden scores remains with in 0.03, except in introduction of a reference answer scored 5.
By comparing the results of Qwen3-32B and Qwen3-8B, it is evident that judge models with larger parameter sizes demonstrate greater robustness in scoring.
In comparison with the GPT-4.1 scores in BiGGen-Bench and Vicuna Bench, the correlation coefficients of the Qwen3-8B scores exhibit maximum fluctuations exceeding 0.05 under score rubrics order bias and score IDs bias.
With reference answer score bias, the maximum fluctuation reaches nearly 0.2.
However, the changes in the correlation coefficient of Qwen3-32B under biases are generally within 0.03.
Therefore, more capable judge models should be prioritized in scoring evaluations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22316v1/ref_ans_cor.svg" id="Sx4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/167;" width="698" height="167"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Spearman’s correlation between human annotator scores and judge model scores with bias in reference answer score on FLASK.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Different judge models exhibit varying sensitivity to scoring bias.</span>
The impact of bias on the scoring accuracy of judge models can be either positive or negative.
However, this impact is not fixed; different judge models have different trends in scoring accuracy when affected by the same bias.
As Table <a href="#Sx4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows, for example, in most cases, the correlation between GPT-4o scores and golden scores increases when Roman numerals are used as scoring IDs.
But when DeepSeek-V3-671B, Qwen3-32B and Qwen3-8B are employed as judge models, the use of Roman numerals generally exerts a negative effect on their scoring accuracy.
An interesting phenomenon is that the impact of reference answer score bias on the judge models we evaluated appears to be consistent, which will be further analyzed in subsequent sections.
Our experimental results can serve as a valuable reference for the selection of judge models and the design of scoring prompt templates.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Judge models have their own scoring tendencies.</span>
Figure <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Judge models are sensitive to scoring biases in input prompt templates. ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents the scoring tendencies of different judge models under various scoring biases on the same dataset FLASK in the form of heatmaps.
It is obvious that each judge model exhibits its own specific scoring preferences.
Under unbiased conditions, DeepSeek-V3-671B assigns a score of 5 to more than half of the instruction-response pairs.
Compared to the human scoring distribution, Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct has a stronger tendency to score a 4 to the samples.
Other judge models show smoother scoring distribution shifts, but certain scores are still preferred.
GPT-4o demonstrates a preference for score 4, while Qwen3-32B favors score 5.
An examination of Figure <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Judge models are sensitive to scoring biases in input prompt templates. ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reveals that score rubrics order bias and score IDs bias have a certain impact on the scoring tendencies of the judge models; however, they do not fundamentally alter the overall score distribution.
An exception is Qwen3-8B, whose score distribution is more significantly influenced by biases, possibly due to its small size.
It is particularly evident that the reference answer score bias exerts a substantial impact on the score distribution, which will be analyzed in detail in the following sections.
Our experimental results show that judge models inherently exhibit biases in their scoring tendencies.
Therefore, debiasing the judge models is crucial to ensuring the fairness and reliability of the evaluation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22316v1/heat_map_arXiv_v1.png" id="Sx4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/128;" width="698" height="128" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Heatmaps of judge models’ score distribution with all bias types on FLASK. The horizontal axis is the score, where -1 represents the failed judgment. The vertical axis is the perturbation method. The top lines of all heatmaps are the human scoring tendencies for comparison, and the second lines are results from unbiased judge models.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Exploratory Experiments and Discussion</h3>

<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">For advanced or large size judge models, application of scoring prompt that differ from usual preferences are worth to be considered.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In almost all scoring-based evaluation tasks, Arabic numerals are commonly used as scoring IDs.
The score rubrics order typically follows a sequential order from score 1 to score 5.
These choices align with human writing conventions and cognitive preferences in the design of scoring prompts.
However, our experimental results shown in Table <a href="#Sx4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> indicate that the scoring prompts designed to align with the usual preferences do not necessarily produce the most accurate scoring results.
As mentioned earlier, the performance of the GPT-4o judge model in scoring evaluations improves when Roman numerals are used as scoring IDs.
In more than half of the cases, using letter grades as scoring Ids improves the scoring accuracy of DeepSeek-V3-671B.
Regarding the score rubrics order bias, adopting a descending order improves the scoring accuracy of GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, and Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct in most instances.
Intuitively, employing a random scoring rubrics order often tends to negatively impact the scoring accuracy of all judge models.
Based on the above analysis, we recommend incorporating certain intentional deviations from conventional practices in the design of scoring prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">The reference answer score bias perform intriguing impacts on the robustness of scoring-based evaluation and play a critical role.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The impact of reference answer score bias on the scoring accuracy and scoring tendency of the judge model is presented in Figure <a href="#Sx4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Judge models are sensitive to scoring biases in input prompt templates. ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Figure <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Judge models are sensitive to scoring biases in input prompt templates. ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
The experimental results indicate that, compared to the score rubrics order bias and the score IDs bias, the influence of the reference answer score bias on scoring robustness is both more pronounced and demonstrates stronger regularity.
Figure <a href="#Sx4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Judge models are sensitive to scoring biases in input prompt templates. ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the impact of introducing reference answers with different scores in the input prompt on the scoring accuracy of the judge model.
For GPT-4o, DeepSeek-V3-671B, and Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct, the scoring accuracy in most cases improves when reference answers are introduced.
However, for the two Qwen judge models, only reference answers with scores of 4 or 5 contribute to enhancing scoring performance.
Figure <a href="#Sx4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Judge models are sensitive to scoring biases in input prompt templates. ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> also reveals an interesting phenomenon: the higher the score of the reference answer, the better the scoring accuracy of the judge model.
Although DeepSeek-V3-671B, Qwen3-32B, and Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct do not strictly adhere to this pattern, the correlation between their scores and human scores similarly exhibits an upward trend as the reference answer score increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Judge models are sensitive to scoring biases in input prompt templates. ‣ Main Results ‣ Experiments ‣ Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that the score of the reference answer has a significant impact on the scoring tendency of the judge model.
Specifically, the scoring results from DeepSeek-V3-671B, Qwen3-32B, and Qwen3-8B appear to be highly influenced by the score of the reference answer.
When the score of the reference answer falls within the range of 1 to 4, these models tend to assign similar scores to the evaluated instruction-response pairs.
Interestingly, when a reference answer with a score of 5 is introduced, the scoring behavior of the judge model appears to become more rational.
This is particularly notable for DeepSeek-V3-671B, which exhibits a strong tendency to assign a score of 5 without bias.
However, the introduction of a reference answer scored at 5 mitigates this phenomenon.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the above analysis reveals that the score of the reference answer introduced in the prompt has a significant impact on the judge model’s scoring behavior.
In particular, when reference answers with a score of 5 are introduced, the models achieve the highest scoring accuracy without disproportionately influencing their scoring preferences.
Therefore, it can be concluded that when incorporating reference answers into the scoring-based judge prompt, <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a full-marked reference answer is the best choice</span>.
Interestingly, this conceptually aligns with the principles of traditional statistical metrics, such as BLEU and ROUGE, which emphasize the critical role of golden answers in evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations and Future Work</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although our work offers notable conclusions and insights, some limitations still remain for future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Mitigating scoring bias needs further exploration.
Although there are no dedicated and fancy methods for addressing scoring bias, intuitive and straightforward approaches, such as scoring multiple times followed by majority voting or averaging the scores, require empirical validation for their effectiveness in mitigating scoring bias.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, although our work demonstrates the existence of scoring bias in LLM-as-a-Judge, the underlying causes of scoring bias remain to be validated.
Approaches such as training data analysis and information flow observation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al. 2025</a>)</cite> may help identify the reason scoring bias exists within or outside the model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Conclusion</h2>

<div id="Sx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose scoring bias in LLM-as-a-Judge, defined as scoring judgments shift when judge models are perturbed.
We design an evaluation framework, including three types of scoring bias: score rubrics order bias, score IDs bias, and reference answer score bias.
We select four LLM-as-a-Judge benchmarks and expand them as our evaluation datasets through data synthesis.
Our evaluation metrics can quantify the impact of scoring bias on the scoring accuracy and scoring tendencies of the judge models.
Our experimental results clearly demonstrate that scoring bias can affect the robustness and reliability of existing scoring-based judge models.
Judge models with different capabilities, sizes, and architectures exhibit different sensitivities to various scoring biases.
Our further exploratory experiments and discussions reveal several intriguing phenomena.
In summary, our work provides detailed evidence and valuable recommendations for evaluating and improving the scoring reliability of LLM-as-a-Judge.</p>
</div>
</section>
